add(1987,'BMO',1,4,'Number theory',r'(a) Find all integer solutions of $a^3+b^3=9$. (b) Find all integer solutions of $35x^3+66x^2y+42xy^2+9y^3=9$.','A factor-pair argument solves the first equation; recognising an invertible linear change of variables reduces the second to it.',None,['Sum of cubes','Diophantine equations','Substitution'],date='1987-03-20')
add(1987,'BMO',2,5,'Geometry',r'In triangle $ABC$, $AB=AC$ and $\angle BAC=100^\circ$. The bisector of $\angle ABC$ meets $AC$ at $D$. Prove $AD+DB=BC$.','The resulting twenty-, forty- and sixty-degree angles call for an auxiliary equilateral construction or a nontrivial sine identity.',None,['Angle bisectors','Special angles','Equilateral constructions'],date='1987-03-20')
add(1987,'BMO',3,4.5,'Algebra',r'Find $\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\sum_{r=0}^{n}\binom{2n}{2r}2^r}{\sum_{r=0}^{n-1}\binom{2n}{2r+1}2^r}$.','Separating the even and odd parts of a binomial expansion converts the ratio of sums into two dominant exponential terms.',r'Let $A=(1+\sqrt2)^{2n}$ and $B=(1-\sqrt2)^{2n}$. The numerator is $(A+B)/2$ and the denominator is $(A-B)/(2\sqrt2)$. Since $B/A\to0$, the ratio tends to $\sqrt2$.',['Binomial theorem','Limits'],date='1987-03-20')
add(1987,'BMO',4,4.5,'Number theory',r'An integer-coefficient polynomial satisfies $P(21)=17,P(32)=-247,P(37)=33$. Prove that any integer $N$ with $P(N)=N+51$ must equal $26$.','Polynomial congruences at two points create a short divisor intersection; the third value eliminates the remaining candidate.',r'Put $Q(x)=P(x)-x-51$. Then $Q(21)=Q(37)=-55$ and $Q(32)=-330$. If $Q(N)=0$, both $N-21$ and $N-37$ divide 55. These two signed divisors differ by 16, so they are $(5,-11)$ or $(11,-5)$, giving $N=26$ or $32$. The second is excluded by $Q(32)\ne0$.',['Polynomial divisibility','Divisors'],date='1987-03-20')
add(1987,'BMO',5,6,'Geometry',r'A line parallel to $BC$ cuts $AB,AC$ of an acute triangle $ABC$ at $F,E$. Prove the circles with diameters $BE,CF$ intersect on the altitude from $A$ to $BC$.','The parallel cut makes the radical axis a specific altitude; proving genuine intersection also requires the acute configuration.',None,['Radical axis','Circles with diameters','Altitudes'],date='1987-03-20')
add(1987,'BMO',6,5.5,'Algebra',r'Find the maximum of $\dfrac{xyz}{(1+x)(x+y)(y+z)(z+16)}$ over positive real $x,y,z$.','Ratios of successive variables turn the denominator into a symmetric product with a fixed product constraint.',r'Put $u=x,v=y/x,w=z/y,t=16/z$, so $uvwt=16$. The reciprocal of the expression is $(1+u)(1+v)(1+w)(1+t)$. Expanding and applying AM–GM separately to terms of each degree gives a lower bound $1+4\cdot2+6\cdot4+4\cdot8+16=81$. Equality occurs at $u=v=w=t=2$, meaning $(x,y,z)=(2,4,8)$. The maximum is $1/81$.',['AM-GM','Substitution','Sharp bounds'],date='1987-03-20')
add(1987,'BMO',7,5,'Number theory',r'Prove that for every positive $n,k$, there is a positive integer $x$ with $x(x+1)/2\equiv k\pmod{2^n}$.','Triangular numbers form a complete residue system modulo a power of two; the parity of the two difference factors establishes injectivity.',r'For $0\le i,j<2^n$, equality of triangular residues implies $2^{n+1}\mid(i-j)(i+j+1)$. The two factors have opposite parity. The even factor must therefore be divisible by $2^{n+1}$, but its absolute value is smaller than $2^{n+1}$, so it must be zero. Only $i-j=0$ is possible. Hence these $2^n$ residues are all distinct and exhaust the modulus. If the selected index is zero, replace it by $2^{n+1}$ to obtain a positive index with the same residue.',['Valuations','Complete residue systems','Triangular numbers'],date='1987-03-20')
add(1988,'BMO',1,4,'Algebra',r'Find all real $x$ satisfying $\sqrt{x+1972098-1986\sqrt{x+986049}}+\sqrt{x+1974085-1988\sqrt{x+986049}}=1$.','The large constants hide two neighbouring completed squares; respecting principal square roots turns the equation into an interval condition.',r'Put $t=\sqrt{x+993^2}\ge0$. The two radicands become $(t-993)^2$ and $(t-994)^2$, so the equation is $|t-993|+|t-994|=1$. This holds exactly for $993\le t\le994$. Thus precisely $0\le x\le994^2-993^2=1987$ work.',['Radicals','Completing squares','Absolute values'],date='1987-11-20')
add(1988,'BMO',2,5,'Algebra',r'Find all real-valued functions on integers $n\ge10$ satisfying $f(x+y)=f(x)f(y)$ whenever $x,y\ge10$.','The missing small arguments prevent the usual direct induction; ratios on the tail must be extended back to the full domain.',None,['Functional equations','Restricted domains'],date='1987-11-20')
add(1988,'BMO',3,4.5,'Number theory',r'Find integers $r,s$ with $0<s<200$ and $59/80<r/s<45/61$, and prove the pair is unique.','The two endpoints are Farey neighbours; a determinant identity turns the denominator bound into a tiny positive-integer equation.',r'Let $a=45s-61r>0$ and $b=80r-59s>0$. Since $45\cdot80-59\cdot61=1$, $s=80a+61b$. With $a,b$ positive and $s<200$, necessarily $a=b=1$: increasing either gives at least 202. Thus $s=141$ and $r=104$. This pair indeed lies strictly between the two bounds.',['Farey fractions','Diophantine equations'],date='1987-11-20')
add(1988,'BMO',4,5.5,'Geometry',r'Let $H$ be the orthocentre of triangle $ABC$, with $\angle BAC\ne90^\circ$. Project $H$ perpendicularly onto the internal and external bisectors of $\angle BAC$ at $P,Q$. Prove $PQ$ passes through the midpoint of $BC$.','The perpendicular bisector axes give simple coordinates for H, but its altitude conditions must connect those coordinates to the side midpoint.',None,['Orthocentre','Projections','Angle bisectors'],date='1987-11-20')
add(1988,'BMO',5,4.5,'Algebra',r'Define $d(n,0)=d(n,n)=1$ for $n\ge0$, and $m\,d(n,m)=m\,d(n-1,m)+(2n-m)d(n-1,m-1)$ for $0<m<n$. Prove every $d(n,m)$ is an integer.','Division by m conceals integrality; recognising the closed form as a square of a binomial coefficient resolves it.',r'In fact $d(n,m)=\binom nm^2$. The boundary values agree. Using $\binom{n-1}{m}=\frac{n-m}{m}\binom{n-1}{m-1}$ and $\binom nm=\frac nm\binom{n-1}{m-1}$, the recurrence reduces to $n^2=(n-m)^2+m(2n-m)$. Thus induction proves the formula and integrality.',['Recurrences','Binomial coefficients'],date='1987-11-20')
add(1988,'BMO',6,5,'Algebra',r'For real $x,y$ satisfying $7x^2+3xy+3y^2=1$, prove the least positive value of $(x^2+y^2)/y$ is $1/2$.','A ratio substitution reduces the ellipse optimisation to a quartic inequality with a hidden square factor.',r'A positive value requires $y>0$. Put $t=x/y$; then $y=1/\sqrt{7t^2+3t+3}$. The square of the expression is $(t^2+1)^2/(7t^2+3t+3)$. The identity $4(t^2+1)^2-(7t^2+3t+3)=(2t-1)^2(t^2+t+1)\ge0$ gives the lower bound $1/2$, attained when $t=1/2$.',['Optimisation','Factorisation','Quadratic forms'],date='1987-11-20')
omit(1989,'BMO',1,3,'Evaluating at x=10 makes the two integer factors multiply to one, leaving two immediate coefficient checks.')
omit(1989,'BMO',2,3.5,'The centroid condition follows directly from the intercept equation of a line in oblique coordinates.')
add(1989,'BMO',3,4,'Geometry',r'Three segments $OA,OB,OC$ are mutually perpendicular. Express the area of triangle $ABC$ in terms of the areas of $OBC,OCA,OAB$.','The three coordinate-plane areas are components of the area vector, giving a spatial Pythagorean identity.',r'Write $OA=a,OB=b,OC=c$. A cross product for two sides of $ABC$ has components $(bc,ca,ab)$, so $[ABC]^2=((bc)^2+(ca)^2+(ab)^2)/4=[OBC]^2+[OCA]^2+[OAB]^2$. Take the positive square root.',['Vectors','Area','Spatial Pythagoras'],date='1988-12-13')
add(1989,'BMO',4,5.5,'Number theory',r'Begin with the row $(1)$. Form each next row by summing each entry with its two neighbours, treating missing positions as zero; the first rows are $(1,1,1)$ and $(1,2,3,2,1)$. Prove every row from the third onward has an even entry.','The rows are trinomial coefficients; parity must rule out an entire row of odd coefficients, rather than one fixed coefficient.',None,['Generating functions','Parity','Trinomial coefficients'],date='1988-12-13')
omit(1989,'BMO',5,3.5,'The sine graph lies above its chord on the acute-angle interval; summing the three chord bounds gives the strict result.')
add(1989,'BMO',6,6,'Number theory',r'An integer sequence starts $a_1=2,a_2=7$ and for $n\ge2$ satisfies $-1/2<a_{n+1}-a_n^2/a_{n-1}\le1/2$. Prove $a_n$ is odd for every $n>1$.','The nearest-integer nonlinear rule hides a simple linear recurrence; proving the approximation error remains below one half is essential.',None,['Recurrences','Rounding','Parity'],date='1988-12-13')
omit(1990,'BMO',1,3,'The requested existence is witnessed by the familiar cyclic decimal number 142857, whose moved leading digit gives its triple.')
add(1990,'BMO',2,5,'Geometry',r'For a square $ABCD$ and $P$ on line $AB$, find the greatest and least possible $PC/PD$. Show the extremal points satisfy $AP\cdot BP=AB^2$.','The ratio of quadratic distance expressions has two external extremisers; a sharp discriminant or derivative calculation identifies both.',None,['Distance ratios','Optimisation','Squares'],date='1990-01-17')
add(1990,'BMO',3,4.5,'Geometry',r'The angles of convex quadrilateral $ABCD$ satisfy $\cos A+\cos B+\cos C+\cos D=0$. Prove the quadrilateral has a pair of parallel opposite sides or is cyclic.','Sum-to-product identities factor the angular condition into adjacent- or opposite-angle supplementarity.',None,['Trigonometric identities','Cyclic quadrilaterals','Parallel lines'],date='1990-01-17')
add(1990,'BMO',4,5.5,'Combinatorics',r'A coin has head probability $p\in(0,1)$. Toss it until HHH or HTH first appears as a consecutive block. One player wins on HHH, the other on HTH. For which $p$ are their winning probabilities equal?','Overlapping patterns mean raw three-toss probabilities are insufficient; a small system of prefix states is needed.',None,['Probability','Recurrences','Pattern matching'],date='1990-01-17')
add(1990,'BMO',5,6,'Geometry',r'Diagonals of convex quadrilateral $ABCD$ meet at $O$. Let $P,Q$ be the centroids of $AOD,BOC$, and let $R,S$ be the orthocentres of $AOB,COD$. Prove $PQ\perp RS$.','The centroids give an easy vector difference, while the two orthocentre equations must be combined along nonperpendicular diagonals.',None,['Vectors','Centroid','Orthocentre'],date='1990-01-17')
add(1990,'BMO',6,5,'Number theory',r'Rational numbers $x,y$ satisfy $x^5+y^5=2x^2y^2$. Prove $1-xy$ is a rational square.','A difference-of-squares identity using the fifth powers extracts the desired square without solving the equation.',r'If either variable is zero, the equation forces both zero and the result is immediate. Otherwise, $(x^5-y^5)^2=(x^5+y^5)^2-4x^5y^5=4x^4y^4(1-xy)$. Thus $1-xy=((x^5-y^5)/(2x^2y^2))^2$, a rational square.',['Rational squares','Algebraic identities'],date='1990-01-17')
omit(1991,'BMO',1,3,'Modulo eight the expression is three or five according to the parity of n, neither a square residue.')
add(1991,'BMO',2,4.5,'Algebra',r'Find positive integers $k$ for which $x^k+x+1$ divides $x^{2k+1}+x+1$. For each such $k$, determine all nonnegative integers $n$ for which it also divides $x^n+x+1$.','Reducing the higher power modulo the proposed divisor produces a fixed cubic; the surviving divisor then has period three.',r'Modulo $x^k+x+1$, the first numerator equals $x(x+1)^2+x+1=(x+1)(x^2+x+1)$. Hence the divisor degree is at most three. Direct checking $k=1,2,3$ leaves only $k=2$. Modulo $x^2+x+1$ we have $x^3=1$, so $x^n+x+1$ vanishes exactly when $n\equiv2\pmod3$.',['Polynomial division','Roots of unity'],date='1991-01-16')
add(1991,'BMO',3,4,'Geometry',r'In a cyclic quadrilateral of circumradius $r$, diagonals meet at $E$. Prove that perpendicular diagonals imply $EA^2+EB^2+EC^2+ED^2=4r^2$. Is the converse true? Justify your answer.','The forward direction uses two right-angle coordinate axes; the converse requires recognising the exceptional case of diameters through the centre.',None,['Cyclic quadrilaterals','Converse statements','Pythagoras'],date='1991-01-16')
add(1991,'BMO',4,4,'Algebra',r'Find the minimum of $(x+y)(y+z)$ for positive real $x,y,z$ satisfying $xyz(x+y+z)=1$.','A regrouping turns the target into a quantity plus its reciprocal; equality must still be realised by positive variables.',r'Put $t=y(x+y+z)$. The constraint gives $xz=1/t$, while $(x+y)(y+z)=t+xz=t+1/t\ge2$. Equality is attained at $x=z=1,y=\sqrt2-1$, so the minimum is 2.',['AM-GM','Substitution','Optimisation'],date='1991-01-16')
add(1991,'BMO',5,5,'Combinatorics',r'Count permutations $(p_1,\ldots,p_6)$ of $1,\ldots,6$ for which no initial segment of length $n\le5$ contains exactly $1,\ldots,n$.','Classifying a permutation by its first closed initial block yields an inclusion-free recurrence for indecomposable permutations.',r'Let $a_n$ count permutations with no proper closed initial segment. Every permutation of $n$ has a unique shortest closed initial segment of length $k$, which can be chosen in $a_k$ ways, followed by an arbitrary permutation of the remaining $n-k$ values. Thus $n!=\sum_{k=1}^n a_k(n-k)!$. Starting with $a_1=1$, this gives $a_2=1,a_3=3,a_4=13,a_5=71,a_6=461$.',['Permutations','Recurrences','Decomposition'],date='1991-01-16')
add(1991,'BMO',6,6.5,'Number theory',r'If positive integers $x,y$ satisfy $2xy\mid x^2+y^2-x$, prove $x$ is a square.','A two-variable Vieta descent preserves the square status of x while reducing the larger coordinate; the asymmetry of the linear term needs care.',None,['Vieta jumping','Descent','Squares'],date='1991-01-16')
add(1991,'BMO',7,5.5,'Geometry',r'A ladder of length $l$ leans against a vertical wall. A point on the ladder is at distance $d$ from both the wall and the horizontal ground. Determine explicitly the possible height $h$ reached by the ladder in terms of $l,d$.','The intercept condition gives the product in terms of the sum of the two intercepts; solving the resulting quadratics requires both possible configurations and feasibility.',None,['Right triangles','Quadratic equations','Ladders'],date='1991-01-16')
