# FIST papers: all numbered questions and printed alternatives are assessed.
add(1972,'FIST',1,4.5,'Combinatorics',r'Label the vertices of a regular $2^n$-gon with all $n$-digit strings over $\{1,2\}$, each used once, so that labels at adjacent vertices differ in exactly one position. Give a construction.','An inductive reflected ordering must preserve adjacency at both joins, including the closing edge.',r'For $n=1$ use the cyclic list $1,2$. Given a suitable list $w_1,\ldots,w_{2^n}$, form $1w_1,\ldots,1w_{2^n},2w_{2^n},\ldots,2w_1$. Within either half, successive strings differ in one position. At the middle join and the closing join, only the first digit changes. Every string occurs exactly once, completing the induction.',['Gray codes','Induction'],date='1972-05-17')
add(1972,'FIST',2,4,'Algebra',r'For positive $a,b,c,d$, prove $\frac{a+c}{a+b}+\frac{b+d}{b+c}+\frac{a+c}{c+d}+\frac{b+d}{d+a}\ge4$, and determine equality.','Pairing opposite denominators exposes two reciprocal-sum inequalities with the same total.',r'Write $T=a+b+c+d$. Since $1/u+1/v\ge4/(u+v)$ for positive $u,v$, the first and third terms sum to at least $4(a+c)/T$, and the other two to at least $4(b+d)/T$. Adding proves the result. Equality requires $a+b=c+d$ and $b+c=d+a$, equivalently $a=c$ and $b=d$.',['Cauchy-Schwarz','Equality cases'],date='1972-05-17')
add(1972,'FIST',3,5.5,'Combinatorics',r'In a group of $n$ people, friendship is mutual. Friends have no common friend, while strangers have exactly two common friends. Prove everyone has the same number of friends. If this number is $5$, determine $n$.','A local matching between the neighbours of adjacent vertices proves regularity; a second count determines the population.',r'Let $u,v$ be friends. Put $A=N(u)\setminus\{v\}$ and $B=N(v)\setminus\{u\}$. For each $a\in A$, the strangers $a,v$ have common friend $u$ and exactly one other common friend in $B$. Similarly every vertex of $B$ has exactly one neighbour in $A$. Thus these edges give a bijection, so $\deg u=\deg v$. Any two strangers are joined by a two-edge path, hence the graph is connected and all degrees are equal, say $k$. Fix a vertex. Its $k$ neighbours have $k(k-1)$ edges to the other $n-k-1$ vertices; each such vertex has exactly two neighbours among them. Thus $k(k-1)=2(n-k-1)$. For $k=5$, this gives $n=16$.',['Graph theory','Regular graphs','Double counting'],date='1972-05-17')
add(1972,'FIST',4,5,'Geometry',r'Let $a=BC,b=CA,c=AB$ for a triangle $ABC$. Determine the locus of points $P$ in three-dimensional space satisfying $aPA^2+bPB^2+cPC^2=kabc$, first for $k=1$ and then for other real $k$.','A weighted centroid identity turns the three squared distances into one; recognising the centre as the incentre completes the locus.',r'Let $T=a+b+c$ and $I=(aA+bB+cC)/T$, the incentre. The weighted variance identity gives $aPA^2+bPB^2+cPC^2=TPI^2+(ab\,AB^2+bc\,BC^2+ca\,CA^2)/T=TPI^2+abc$. Hence $k=1$ gives just $I$; $k>1$ gives the sphere centred at $I$ with radius $\sqrt{(k-1)abc/T}$; and $k<1$ gives no points.',['Weighted centroids','Loci','Incentre'],date='1972-05-17')
add(1974,'FIST',1,5,'Geometry',r'(i) If a triangle satisfies $\sin^2A+\sin^2B+\sin^2C=2$, prove it is right-angled. (ii) With circumcentre $O$, centroid $G$ and circumradius $R$, prove $9OG^2=R^2(1-8\cos A\cos B\cos C)$.','The trigonometric identity for three triangle angles must be combined with the centroid-distance formula.',None,['Triangle identities','Centroid','Circumcentre'],notes='The scan credits part (i) to Hungary (modified) and part (ii) to Bulgaria (modified), without years. This entry records the verified UK FIST appearance.')
add(1974,'FIST',2,6,'Geometry',r'A polygonal path lies in a square of side $50$. Every point inside the square is at distance less than $1$ from the path. Prove that the path has length greater than $1248$.','Bounding the area swept by a unit neighbourhood requires controlling the overlaps at bends and the endpoint caps.',None,['Area bounds','Geometric covering'],notes='The scan credits the USSR, without a year; this entry records the verified UK FIST appearance.')
add(1974,'FIST',3,6.5,'Geometry',r'A circular hoop of radius $1$ lies in a corner formed by three mutually perpendicular planes, touching all three planes and staying within the corner. Find the locus of its centre.','The projection radius of a tilted circle converts the three tangencies into a spherical equation with coordinate restrictions.',None,['Solid geometry','Loci','Projections'],notes='The scan credits France, without a year; this entry records the verified UK FIST appearance.')
add(1974,'FIST','4a',4.5,'Number theory',r'Prove that the cube roots of three distinct primes cannot be three terms, even nonconsecutive terms, of an arithmetic progression.','An integer linear relation between the cube roots can be cubed using the sum-of-three-cubes identity, forcing an impossible rational cube root.',r'If the three numbers occur in an arithmetic progression, there are nonzero integers $a,b,c$ such that $a\sqrt[3]p+b\sqrt[3]q+c\sqrt[3]r=0$. Set $x=a\sqrt[3]p,y=b\sqrt[3]q,z=c\sqrt[3]r$. The identity $x^3+y^3+z^3=3xyz$ when $x+y+z=0$ shows $3abc\sqrt[3]{pqr}$ is an integer. But $\sqrt[3]{pqr}$ is irrational, since the three primes each occur with exponent one. This is a contradiction.',['Prime factorisation','Cubic identities','Arithmetic progressions'],notes='Printed alternative 4(a). The scan credits the USA, without a year; this entry records the UK FIST appearance.')
add(1974,'FIST','4b',4.5,'Algebra',r'Real numbers $x,y,z$ satisfy $x+y+z=3$, $x^3+y^3+z^3=15$, $x^4+y^4+z^4=35$, and $x^2+y^2+z^2<10$. Find $x^5+y^5+z^5$.','Newton sums produce a two-valued symmetric sum; the extra inequality selects the correct branch.',r'Let $e_2=xy+yz+zx$, $e_3=xyz$ and $p_j=x^j+y^j+z^j$. Then $p_2=9-2e_2$ and $15=27-9e_2+3e_3$, so $e_3=3e_2-4$. Also $35=3p_3-e_2p_2+3e_3=33+2e_2^2$, giving $e_2=\pm1$. Since $p_2<10$, $e_2=1$, hence $p_2=7,e_3=-1$. Therefore $p_5=3p_4-e_2p_3+e_3p_2=105-15-7=83$.',['Newton sums','Symmetric polynomials'],notes='Printed alternative 4(b). The scan credits the GDR, modified, without a year; this entry records the UK FIST appearance.')
add(1975,'FIST',1,4.5,'Algebra',r'(i) Determine all constants $b$ for which a function $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ can satisfy $f(x-y)=f(x)-f(y)+bxy$ for every $x,y$. (ii) Suppose $f(x+y)=f(x)+f(y)+cxy$ for a real constant $c$. Prove continuity at $0$ implies continuity everywhere. Prove differentiability at $0$ implies differentiability everywhere, and determine $f$ in this case.','Substitution handles the first part; a difference quotient propagates regularity and identifies the quadratic correction.',None,['Functional equations','Continuity','Differentiation'],notes='Historical selection problem requiring elementary calculus in its final part. Statement paraphrased from the official scan.',date='1975-05-05')
add(1975,'FIST',2,5,'Number theory',r'Let $S=\{3\cdot4^k,-2\cdot4^k:k\ge0\}$. Prove every positive integer outside $S$ is a sum of two or more members of $S$.','A signed base-four expansion supplies the representation; the two generators at each scale cover all four residues.',r'We prove the stronger assertion that every nonnegative integer is a sum of distinct members of $S$, allowing the empty sum for zero. For $n>0$, select $d\in\{0,1,-2,3\}$ congruent to $n$ modulo 4. Then $m=(n-d)/4$ is a nonnegative integer smaller than $n$. By induction represent $m$, and multiply its representation by 4. Add nothing for $d=0$, the terms $3,-2$ for $d=1$, the term $-2$ for $d=-2$, or $3$ for $d=3$. The added terms have a new scale, so remain distinct. If $n\notin S$, a representation cannot contain just one term, completing the proof.',['Signed digits','Induction','Base representations'],date='1975-05-05')
add(1975,'FIST',3,7,'Combinatorics',r'Two contestants from each of $n$ countries queue in uniformly random order. The first enters room $A$. Each later contestant enters the same room as the preceding contestant unless their compatriot is already there, in which case they enter the other room. Find, with proof, the probability that room $A$ receives its $n$th contestant before room $B$ does.','The final-room event depends on alternating runs controlled by first and second appearances of countries; a symmetry or pairing argument must track this dependence.',None,['Probability','Random permutations','Pairing'],date='1975-05-05')
omit(1975,'FIST',4,7.5,'Realising the specified twelve-circle tangency graph by equal circles on a sphere requires a substantial polyhedral construction and spherical-distance verification.')
omit(1976,'FIST',1,3.5,'Barycentric coordinates make each median-parallel intercept a linear expression; the three expressions sum immediately to a constant.')
add(1976,'FIST',2,4,'Algebra',r'For $n\ge2$, all coefficients $a_2,\ldots,a_n$ lie in $[-1,1]$. Prove every real root $t$ of $x^n+a_2x^{n-2}+a_3x^{n-3}+\cdots+a_n=0$ satisfies $|t|\le(1+\sqrt5)/2$.','The absent next-to-leading coefficient changes the usual root bound to the golden ratio via a geometric series.',r'Put $u=|t|$. If $u\le1$ there is nothing to prove. Otherwise $u^n\le1+u+\cdots+u^{n-2}=(u^{n-1}-1)/(u-1)<u^{n-1}/(u-1)$. Hence $u(u-1)<1$, so in fact $u<(1+\sqrt5)/2$.',['Polynomial root bounds','Geometric series'],date='1976-05-05')
add(1976,'FIST',3,5.5,'Number theory',r'Prove $x^2-3y^2+5z^2-7t^2=0$ has only the zero integer solution. Prove $x^2-3y^2-5z^2+7t^2=0$ has infinitely many positive integer solutions, no two with the same ratio $x:y:z:t$.','The first quadratic form yields a parity descent; the sign change in the second permits a Pell family.',r'For the first equation, reduction modulo 4 forces either all variables even or all odd. If all are odd, their squares are 1 modulo 8 and the left side is $1-3+5-7\equiv4\pmod8$, impossible. Thus all are even, and dividing by 4 repeatedly excludes any nonzero solution. For the second equation set $z=t=1$. Define positive integers $x_k,y_k$ by $x_k+y_k\sqrt3=(1+\sqrt3)(2+\sqrt3)^k$ for $k\ge0$. Conjugating gives $x_k^2-3y_k^2=-2$, so these solve the equation. The $x_k$ strictly increase, and the final two coordinates are fixed at 1, so no two ratios agree.',['Infinite descent','Pell equations','Parity'],date='1976-05-05')
add(1976,'FIST',4,4,'Number theory',r'Prove no positive integers $p,q$ satisfy $\left|p/q-\sqrt7\right|\le2/(11q^2)$.','Multiplying by the conjugate traps a nonzero integer between zero and one, but the numerical bound must be kept tight.',r'If the bound held, then $p/q\le\sqrt7+2/11$, and therefore $0<|p^2-7q^2|\le(2/11)(2\sqrt7+2/11)$. Since $\sqrt7<53/20$, the right-hand side is less than $53/55+4/121=603/605<1$, impossible for a positive integer.',['Irrational approximation','Conjugates'],date='1976-05-05')
add(1976,'FIST',5,7,'Geometry',r'Two externally tangent equal circles form a figure-eight curve. Construct two distinct congruent hexagons whose vertices all lie on this curve, each using both circles, such that no single translation, rotation or reflection carries one hexagon to the other. The hexagons need not be convex.','A glide reflection must be realised using a carefully chosen intersection pattern of the circles, while excluding accidental alternative symmetries.',None,['Isometries','Glide reflections','Constructions'],date='1976-05-05')
add(1977,'FIST',1,6.5,'Algebra',r'Let $-1=x_0<x_1<\cdots<x_k=1$, with every gap at most $1/2$. A real quadratic polynomial $f$ satisfies $|f(x_i)|\le1$ for all $i$. Prove $|f(x)|\le17/15$ throughout $[-1,1]$, and prove this constant is best possible.','The vertex position and nearest mesh points must be balanced against the endpoint constraints to obtain the sharp overshoot.',None,['Quadratic polynomials','Sharp inequalities','Interpolation'],date='1977-05-11')
add(1977,'FIST',2,7,'Geometry',r'A pyramid has planar quadrilateral base $ABCD$ and apex $V$. The incircles of every pair of adjacent triangular faces touch one another. Prove the four points where those incircles touch the base plane are concyclic.','Matching tangent lengths on the lateral edges creates a shared spatial metric condition for the four contact points.',None,['Tangent lengths','Spatial geometry','Concyclicity'],date='1977-05-11')
omit(1977,'FIST','3a',3.5,'Choose x=12 and z=-7 so that the coefficient of y is 10x+17z=1; every integer right-hand side then follows directly.')
omit(1977,'FIST','3b',3.5,'The terms a(1+d)^k all belong to the progression and have the fixed prime divisors of a(1+d), giving a short explicit construction.')
add(1977,'FIST',4,6,'Combinatorics',r'For $n>1$, prove a circular necklace with $2n^2$ beads in $2n$ colours can have every pair of distinct colours appearing on adjacent beads. Prove $2n^2-1$ beads cannot suffice.','The necklace is an Euler tour of a multigraph; odd vertex degrees explain exactly the extra edges needed beyond the complete graph.',r'Use one vertex for each colour. A necklace gives a connected multigraph with even degrees, with an edge for each adjacent pair (loops contribute degree two). Since it must contain $K_{2n}$, whose vertices all have odd degree, the additional edges must change the parity at all $2n$ vertices. At least $n$ extra edges are required, so at least $\binom{2n}{2}+n=2n^2$ beads are needed. Conversely add a second copy of every edge of a perfect matching to $K_{2n}$. The resulting connected graph has all degrees even, so an Euler circuit reads off a necklace with exactly $2n^2$ beads and every required adjacency.',['Euler tours','Graph parity','Extremal constructions'],date='1977-05-11')
# Restore all reviewed historical difficulties, preserving source holds.
